A||N||20||Monkeyman||E brake handle as auto shift lever||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||03:38 PM||||I'm trying to figure out a way to use my E brake handle as an auto tranny shift lever.<P>I have removed both from the Monkeymobile.  Here's what I see.  I'll need at least one and maybe 2 cables from the E brake/shifter (now referred to as the "EBS") to the tranny, some sort of bracket on the EBS end to hold the cable(s), and a gate plate (the thing with detents that keeps you from accidentally shifting into either Reverse or Park while moving forward).<P>What else do I need?<P>I'm not worried about the Park interlock.  I'm not worried about accidentally leaving it in a gear for two reasons.  First, I could probably use the existing idiot/brake light to let me know if it wasn't in Park.  Second, assuming that Park would be with the handle all the way to the floor, it would be obvious that I wasn't in Park when I tried to step out of the car.<P>It was suggested that I could use a single cable with a spring at the tranny end so that it would want to pull the tranny/shifter back into Park.  (You know what I mean.)  Here's a pic of the EBS.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Ebrakelever.JPG"> <P>Looks to me like my gate plate problem would be solved if I cut/ground the teeth off of the black thing (that's a technical term) and notched it for PRND21.  I would need to find some way to mount something where the spring assy is that would engage the black thing and could be pushed down by the other thing that is pushed by the button on top of the lever.  (Hope I'm not confusing anyone with all of my technical terms. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>Someone make some suggestions.  If I can get the stock shifter out of the console, this will open up new and exciting possibilities for my dash.<P>Thanx for the help and the bandwidth.  <P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||10-01-2000||04:25 PM||||Doesn't have to be a "cable", does it ?<P>The throttle on my lawnmower is a solid wire encased in a sheath that both pushes and pulls the throttle plate. Seems like there has to be a similar type "cable" that would work in this application and would eliminate the need for two cables and the spring at the tranny end.<P>I'm also not sure of the need for a lockout or a detent, unless you're one of those crazies (LOL) that thinks he shifts better than his automatic (no flames, please).<P>Seems like the placement of the E brake handle would tend to minimize inadvertent shifts, especially in a car with power windows.<P>No reason why it couldn't work...just depends on what you want to give up (other than money) to make it work.<P>Other than that, gotta be the damn silliest idea I've ever run across. Whoever came up with this oughta be locked away (LOL)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||10-01-2000||04:25 PM||||DO WHAT?||||reg||5
Z||000003||stimpy||10-01-2000||05:29 PM||||Dave, the diet must be working great. I know with my wide a$$, just finding the ebrake with the door closed is a challnge.  What's next, the good ole Saab key in the floor conversion? Howzabout overhead ashtrays? There's gotta be all kinds of ways to expensively mess up the Fieros ergonmics.  If it is just a matter of getting the console cleared off, I'd see if I couldn't find some chevette with a column shift and put it in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||10-01-2000||06:06 PM||||HAHAHAHAHA!!!  overhead ash treys...... hahahahaha....||||reg||10
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||06:46 PM||||Let's see...<P>deceler8--No, it doesn't.  I just had to have some place to start.  If I thought I shifted better than my auto, I'd buy a stick.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Although the placement of the EBS (gotta get these technical terms right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) <I>would</I> tend to minimize inadvertent shifts, I'd still like the security of knowing that I can't shift into R or P without the drivers permission.  I don't think I'd be giving up anything (not much money, either) since I can't use my E brake anyhow.  Actually, it is a silly idea.  So was the gasoline engine.<P>My7Fieros--It's called customization and innovation.  Leading instead of following.  You'll get the picture once it's successful.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>stimpy--Yes, the diet is going well.  Down another 4# last time I checked.  No Saab key ignition on the floor, though.  Sorry to disappoint you.  I am thinking of changing the stock steering column to one from Ididit.  Tilt, turn signals and nothing else.  Hidden ignition switch under the dash, maybe, and a cool lighted starter button a la the Honda S2000 (there--now you made me go and say the "H" word!).  No overhead ashtrays.  As a matter of fact, probably no ashtrays at all.  That's what the window is for.  Expensive?  I doubt it.  I think I could do it today if I had a long enough cable and a spring.  I'm guessing less than $50 unless I have to get the EBS machined.  <P>Laugh if you want, guys.  If I get this (and my custom dash/console) done, you'll be drooling.  I guess I'll just email certain people next time I need advice.<P>Sorry for the bandwidth.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 10-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||10-01-2000||06:53 PM||||They laughed at Christopher Columbus<BR>too. (Until he started giving the Native Americans smallpox and VD, but that's beside the point). Good luck to ya, but I still think a cut-down column shift would be more practical and safer. I cut down the column shift on my last 2 GM ghetto sleds to about a 6" length. Far less conspicuous then you might thnk.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula||10-01-2000||07:20 PM||||That sounds like its going to be a cool dash!!!!!!!  if you do start emailing about its updates, put me on the list PLEASE!!!<P><BR>(I dont live too far away so I might want to come look at it when your done!!!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-01-2000||07:37 PM||||MM. there are detents in both the tranny and the shifter.<P>The ones in the tranny simply provide alighment for parts inside the tranny. They don't do any gear lockout or anything else.<P>The other one in the shifter is what the button works against. The best thing to do is move the lever and play with the button. You can watch whats going on. You have to duplicate all that into the brake lever. keep in mind the shift cable is a push/pull the e brake cable is ony a pull.||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||10-01-2000||10:12 PM||||Just giving you a hard time, Monkey.<P>The act of getting the trans into the gear you want via a cable and lever is not rocket science...it's all the safety stuff that we've all been convinced we need (or must have) is where this gets difficult. Then again, routing the cable from the E-brake to the trans might be a pain in the arse as well.<P>I remember reading in my Dodge Shadow manual once about how the auto trans floor shifter was not interlocked to the steering column and that "children should be cautioned against touching the shift lever". Apparently that one could be moved into any gear even with the engine off and I never recall hearing of a rash of Shadow rollaways.<P>As long as you're the only person who's gonna be driving this and you're not concerned about inspections, go for it. And once you're done, folks will look longingly at your custom shifter and wonder why they didn't think of it. And your dash will probably look great without that auto shifter that really looks so 80's GM.<P>One thing I was thinking about was the inspection angle...if Park is in the fully down position, it won't look right, but if Park is at an extended position, it will look as if your parking brake is functional. But then again, your shifter will be missing, so you'll probably be explaining that anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000010||BOILERMAKER||10-01-2000||10:39 PM||||I think that your rash (I mean plethora, not an itchy skin problem) of Fiero ideas is very exciting.  I must admit that I am quite intrigued at the idea of having the shifter at the left instead of at the right.  Yes, you are right that the space where the shifter is would have great promise when it comes to new toys to add to your ride.<P>I don't really see a problem with the concept of your idea.  All I see is a little elbow grease getting the job done.  As far as machining, I don't really think that you would have to take it anywhere to get your idea on track.  Of course, the first step is usually the hardest, isn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000011||JetroGT||10-02-2000||03:57 AM||||If your going to make it all custom, why not design your own electric shifter? Set up a simple TTL circuit. Selinoids doing the work. Then if you want to put it in drive, just press the D button. <BR>Maybe I'm crazy. But that would be cool.||||reg||12
Z||000012||Sillhouette.com||10-02-2000||04:07 AM||||Why don't you get a shifter with a ratcheting action.  It would work like those newer automatic standard transmissions in cars like the Acura 3.2 TL.  To get your intended gear you have to click it backwards or forrward.  I think B&M sells it for other cars like Camaro and Corvette.  I don't know how hard it would be to modify for the Fiero?  This would give you the clearance for the intended stereo install.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000013||StuGood||10-02-2000||03:33 PM||||Well, here are a few observations / opinions:<P>If you're not going to use the stock shifter setup, think I would lean towards using an electrically-operated one.  Probably use a motor + lead screw.  It'd be tough to make a solenoid work, because it ain't much force (literally) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>An electric system offers more flexibility -you can mount the control pretty much wherever you want, and can be configured pretty much as you wish (pushbuttons or a lever, twist grip, TV remote - <B>J/K</B>??).  Especially important if, like me, you tend to change your mind a lot, which I do.  Well, not a <B>lot</B>, but, sometimes.  Okay, then - a lot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Whichever way you go, think an indicator of some kind would be a good idea.  An electric indicator would be pretty easy and cheap (LEDs or bulbs).     ||||reg||2
Z||000014||hugh||10-02-2000||07:55 PM||||MM<BR>I have a 2 engine 70 Chevelle and was going to put a gear indicator on the rear 425a transmission.I didn't use it and this thread reminded me of it.It uses a 2 1/16 gauge with 6 positions,P,R,N,D,S,L that is electrically operated.It has a sending unit that mounts on the trans.It's from Ron Francis wire works.If you are successful with your shifter I will donate it to the cause.Forget that;I'll send it to you now,and if you don't use it you can send it back.Again this is only an indicator not a shifter. ||||reg||14
Z||000015||GodSend||10-02-2000||10:40 PM||||  Why not put a small computer with some voice rec. software and make it voice activated? - Easy to put in a nav/mp3 system after that.<P>  Mount a laptop under a seat.... run a network connection outside and mount it dead center in the gas door... nice way to exchange data with your car.. or go wireless.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||DaRkLoRD||10-02-2000||10:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GodSend:<BR><B>  Why not put a small computer with some voice rec. software and make it voice activated? - Easy to put in a nav/mp3 system after that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just imagine the headlines...<P><B>BLUE SCREEN OF DEATH RESULTS IN ACCIDENTAL SHIFTING!</B><P>LOL.. crash windows and it accidentally shifts into reverse at 75mph..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000017||pHoOl||10-03-2000||12:46 AM||||Hey, sounds like a cool idea.  When you do get pics goin, i'd be interested in seeing them.  I had a thought too.  Maybe there would be a way to make park all the way up on the lever.  Then keep the action that lets you push the lever down without taking it out of park.  That'd be really cool, but i suppose you might run into problems at first remembering if you set it in park when it's all the way down (presumeably drive).  Anyway, i'm kind curious about the push button idea.  That'd be really cool, and you could figure out a variety of different places to put the buttons.<P>Anyway, i just realized that i was thinking aloud and i'll return you all to the thread.  And btw, i sometimes manually shift with my auto.  I can let the engine run up to the 5500 that i've heard is optimal.  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||10-03-2000||08:59 AM||||Hehehe... I really like the voice-operated trans. idea.  You're taking your girl out on a picnic-lunch date, and...<P>"So - where're we going?" says she.<BR>"To the park..." says he.<P>"Screeeeeeee....ch!" says the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> .<P>Seriously, no matter how smart you are (even if you're a Hoosier), or how careful you are <B>most</B> of the time, it's extremely important to make it goof-proof.  I should know - I'm a highly qualified goof, myself.  So if you need a "goof" tester, I'm your man.<P>Or, like I always say, "Eleven cuidado."   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000019||deceler8||10-04-2000||04:36 PM||||Hope you're still gonna go for it...seems like the cheapest and easiest alternative to the console shifter...<P>A column shift...not sure how that would work as there probably are no columns with shifters that are compatible, and even if there were, you'd still have the problem of making it work on a car that never came with a column shifter.<P>The hydraulic/electronic ideas just seem like they'd cost too damn much. <P>A Hurst ratcheting floor shifter would still take up room in the console area.<P>So, you either go with the console shifter or innovate. My guess is with the help of a trans guy and a machinist you could make it work. Sadly, I'm neither.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||mrfixit58||10-04-2000||05:26 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>I'm a little  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> . Are you trying to move the shift control to your left hip via the X-emergency brake (vis-a-vis Australia and Great Britton)? Or, are you trying to make your shifter look like the 71 Roadrunner pistal grip shift handle? The later would be simple. The former, while possible, could be dangerous (refer to the safety concerns in a previous post).<P>The push-button thing reminds me of the old Plymouth Valiant. Not that it's a bad thing but if you do this you should consider mounting it vertically on the dash. If you mount it horizontal I can picture you cruzing down the highway about 70 MPH then, your cell phone rings. While you reach over to grab the phone it slips from you grip and lands on the "R" button. From there, the vision get ugly...<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
